WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:05.620
Removal shows that a signal is needed.

2
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:07.840
Can it show what the signal says?

3
00:00:08.883 --> 00:00:11.203
The fuel in a car is needed for the trip.

4
00:00:11.823 --> 00:00:13.643
But it does not choose where the car goes.

5
00:00:14.667 --> 00:00:18.307
A permissive signal allows an outcome that something else chooses.

6
00:00:19.147 --> 00:00:21.767
An instructive signal chooses the outcome itself.

7
00:00:22.850 --> 00:00:25.430
Removing a signal cannot tell the two apart.

8
00:00:26.230 --> 00:00:30.590
One test of a spatial message: keep the signal present, but change where it is.

9
00:00:36.483 --> 00:00:41.263
Three simple proposals: stop here, let go of the glial fiber, or come here.

10
00:00:42.117 --> 00:00:47.277
Now move the Reelin: below the cortex, into one small spot, or into the medium

11
00:00:47.277 --> 00:00:48.277
bathing a slice.

12
00:00:49.100 --> 00:00:53.540
Predict. If Reelin means come here, what should happen in each case?

13
00:00:59.583 --> 00:01:05.163
In 2002, Curran's laboratory made Reelin from the ventricular zone of reeler mice.

14
00:01:06.233 --> 00:01:10.413
The preplate split, and the ataxia was rescued, though not every defect.

15
00:01:11.493 --> 00:01:13.533
Neurons did not migrate down toward the source.

16
00:01:14.583 --> 00:01:19.883
In 2010, Nakajima's laboratory made Reelin in one small patch of mouse cortex.

17
00:01:20.983 --> 00:01:25.763
Later-born neurons passed earlier-born ones toward the spot, building a small

18
00:01:25.763 --> 00:01:27.443
inside-out arrangement around it.

19
00:01:28.333 --> 00:01:34.133
In 2004, Jossin, Goffinet and colleagues added Reelin to the medium bathing reeler

20
00:01:34.133 --> 00:01:35.133
slices.

21
00:01:35.583 --> 00:01:40.363
The developing cortical plate became more organized, without Reelin from its normal

22
00:01:40.363 --> 00:01:44.703
source. Whether Reelin stayed even inside the tissue was not shown.

23
00:01:45.733 --> 00:01:48.133
No single command fits all three results.

24
00:01:59.767 --> 00:02:01.427
When does a neuron read Reelin?

25
00:02:02.247 --> 00:02:04.007
Follow a typical later-born neuron.

26
00:02:04.887 --> 00:02:08.307
First it is multipolar, wandering below the cortical plate.

27
00:02:09.467 --> 00:02:12.587
Then it takes hold of a radial glial fiber, and climbs.

28
00:02:13.617 --> 00:02:18.537
Near the top, it anchors its leading process in the marginal zone and pulls its cell

29
00:02:18.537 --> 00:02:20.857
body up: somal translocation.

30
00:02:21.900 --> 00:02:27.160
Reelin signalling, through Dab1 and Rap1, has been linked to both the turn and that

31
00:02:27.160 --> 00:02:28.160
final step.

32
00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.600
On one view, Reelin acts less like a command, and more like a switch on adhesion,

33
00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:35.200
flipped at particular stages.

34
00:02:36.350 --> 00:02:41.290
The essential cue, a gradient, a boundary or an adhesive state, is not settled.

35
00:02:42.333 --> 00:02:47.493
Takeaway: Reelin signalling helps each new wave tend to finish its climb at the top.

36
00:02:48.353 --> 00:02:51.073
That alone does not show that neurons steer toward Reelin.
